From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hades <15710513+hadesdevls@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 26 Nov 2026 18:05:34 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] drm/amd/display: DCE: the Dolby Vision tunnel feeds its
 blender through, turns off what DC never programs, and reads back every stage
 up to the HDMI encoder

Issue #20 of LibreELEC Hades (SunnySnacks): an RX 550 (Polaris 12, DCE 11.2)
on CRTC 0 shows the Dolby Vision logo over a black picture. Its check of the
output (hades-output-check, 1.2.2) finds each component changed on its own,
the same at every frame, every bit reaching the CRC, and the values that any
widening of 8 bits keeps linear (k * 0x11 and k * 0x55) broken too: a curve,
a gain or a rounding of each component. Every register that the read back of
the tunnel judges was as the tunnel needs (linux-9969, 9970, 9885).

Between the plane and the HDMI encoder the pipe of DCE 11.2 and 12 has three
stages that can change a component and that the tunnel left as DC or the
VBIOS had them:

- the blender of the pipe. dce_set_blender_mode() writes, for the current
  pipe alone, BLND_MODE 0, alpha mode 2, no multiplied mode and
  BLND_FEEDTHROUGH_EN 1, except on blender 0 (and on the underlay blender of
  DCE 11.0), which it leaves at 0. That exception comes with the underlay pipe
  of DCE 11.0, which blends into blender 0; the dce112 and dce120 pools create
  no underlay pipe, and the tunnel refuses a second plane. The RX 550 runs the
  tunnel on CRTC 0, so on blender 0, the one blender that is not fed through.
  dce_set_blender_byte_tunnel(), called by program_surface_visibility() right
  after dce_set_blender_mode(), gives a tunnel whose pipe shows its own plane
  alone what DC programs for blenders 1 to 5: the current pipe fed through.
  BLND_CONTROL needs no giving back: DC writes its four fields again at every
  programming of the pipe;
- BLND_CONTROL2 of that blender, with BLND_SUPERAA_DEGAMMA_EN and
  BLND_SUPERAA_REGAMMA_EN, a curve per component each, which DC never
  programs on DCE: both off for the tunnel, the register as the tunnel found
  it written back once the pipe goes on without it;
- the colour keyer of the line buffer (LB_KEYER_COLOR_CTRL: LB_KEYER_COLOR_EN
  and LB_KEYER_COLOR_REP_EN, which replaces a keyed colour), which DC never
  programs either: off for the tunnel and given back the same way
  (dce_transform_set_byte_tunnel(), from dce110_program_front_end_for_pipe()).

Which of them changed the RX 550's bytes is not known from the code: none of
their effects is documented. Each is set to what does not change a pixel by
its name or by what DC itself programs elsewhere, and is read back.

dc_stream_read_byte_tunnel() now also reads every other stage up to the
encoder, and amdgpu_dm judges them (amdgpu_dm_dvbridge_dce_stages_why()) on a
second kernel log line: for each stage that the tunnel sets, what it found,
what it set and what reads back; the surface as program_grph_pixel_format()
and program_tiling() write it for XRGB8888 (32 bpp, format 0, zero expansion,
no crossbar); the cursor off; the subsampling and the chroma bit reduction
bypass of the formatter as set_pixel_encoding() writes them for RGB (0); and
the HDMI link at 8 bpc RGB without AV mute, as
dce110_stream_encoder_hdmi_set_stream_attribute() writes it (deep colour off,
depth 0, TMDS pixel encoding 0, colour format 0). The DCP keyer (it makes an
alpha, which a blender fed through does not use), the alpha control, the
scaler's bypass mode, sharpness and rounding offset (the scaler is in bypass,
SCL_MODE 0, judged before) and the formatter's source CRTC are read, not
judged. A stage that is not as the tunnel needs, or does not answer, refuses
the tunnel as the read back of linux-9994 does: the video goes on as HDR10.
The new registers are listed for DCE 11.2 and 12 only
(HWSEQ_BLND_TUNNEL_REG_LIST(), XFM_DCE112_TUNNEL_REG_LIST()); the log buffers
of amdgpu_dm_dvbridge_log_dce() move off the stack.

Without the tunnel nothing changes: a host test builds the display core's
own files (dce_ipp.c, dce_transform.c, dce_opp.c, dce_mem_input.c,
dce_stream_encoder.c, dce_hwseq.c, dce110_hwseq.c with
program_surface_visibility(), dc_resource.c, dc_hw_sequencer.c, the dce112
and dce120 pools) on a fake register file: seven ordinary scenarios on
DCE 11.2, 11.22, 12.0 and 12.1, with and without update bits, write the same
8280 registers, in the same order and from the same functions, before and
after this patch. With the tunnel, blender 0 is fed through, its SuperAA
curves and the keyer are off with what a VBIOS left elsewhere in them kept,
and they are as the VBIOS left them once the tunnel ends, also after a
suspend; the read back finds each stage changed in its own register; 22474
checks, and mutations of each part of this patch make the test fail.

Not verified: that a DCE does what the names of these fields say, nor which
of them changed the bytes of the RX 550. The next check of its output says
whether the tunnel is exact now, and the kernel log says each stage.
